Based on my investigation of the accessibility issues, here are the primary problems and their solutions:

### Missing Accessible Name (Button)
The switch button (`button.eBlXUe-scr2fc`) has no inner text, `aria-label`, or `aria-labelledby` attribute. A screen reader user will hear "switch" but won't know what it controls.
*   **Fix**: Add an `aria-label` to the button.
    ```html
    <!-- Example fix -->
    <button class="eBlXUe-scr2fc" role="switch" aria-label="بایگانی خودکار">...</button>
    ```

### Invalid ARIA Relationship
A `div` uses `aria-labelledby`, but lacks a valid ARIA role. ARIA labeling attributes are ignored by browsers on generic `div` elements unless they have a role that supports labels.
*   **Fix**: Add `role="group"` or `role="region"` to the `div`.
    ```html
    <!-- Example fix -->
    <div role="region" aria-labelledby="auto-archive-toggle">...</div>
    ```

### Invalid Menu Structure
The `menubar` container has direct children (`<a>` and `<button>`) that do not have the required menu-related roles. This breaks the expected keyboard navigation for screen reader users.
*   **Fix**: The direct children of a `menubar` must have `role="menuitem"`.
    ```html
    <!-- Example fix -->
    <a role="menuitem" aria-label="بازگشت">...</a>
    ```

### Zooming Disabled
The page uses `user-scalable="no"` in the viewport meta tag, which prevents users with low vision from zooming in to see content more clearly.
*   **Fix**: Update the `<meta name="viewport">` tag in the `<head>` to allow scaling.
    ```html
    <!-- Change this -->
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, user-scalable=no">
    <!-- To this -->
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    ```

